  "live_records": {
    "q-124208": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124208",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Which of the following affine transformations are invertible?\n\nI. ${T}_{1}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}6u+3v+3 \\\\ 10u+5v\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. ${T}_{2}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}1-3u-4v \\\\ 3u+4v-2\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. ${T}_{3}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4-6u-4v \\\\ 8u+7v-7\\end{aligned}]$",
      "worked_solution": "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6u+3v+3 \\\\ 10u+5v\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6u+3v \\\\ 10u+5v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}|=5\\cdot 6-3\\cdot 10=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{1}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1-3u-4v \\\\ 3u+4v-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3u-4v \\\\ 3u+4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|=4\\cdot (-3)-(-4)\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{2}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4-6u-4v \\\\ 8u+7v-7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6u-4v \\\\ 8u+7v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{aligned}|=7\\cdot (-6)-(-4)\\cdot 8=-10\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{3}$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{3}$ only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
