    "q-124209": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124209",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Which of the following affine transformations are invertible?\n\nI. ${T}_{1}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-2u-5v-9 \\\\ 4u+9v-9\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. ${T}_{2}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3u+3v+4 \\\\ 3u+3v-1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. ${T}_{3}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3u+v \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$",
      "worked_solution": "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2u-5v-9 \\\\ 4u+9v-9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2u-5v \\\\ 4u+9v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 9\\end{aligned}|=9\\cdot (-2)-(-5)\\cdot 4=2\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{1}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+3v+4 \\\\ 3u+3v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+3v \\\\ 3u+3v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}|=3\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{2}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+v \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+v \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|=0\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{3}$ is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{1}$ only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
